{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a605440", "query": "Prove definitely whether a self-improving artificial intelligence system can be mathematically guaranteed to maintain alignment with human values indefinitely through recursive self-modification.", "system": "You are the blind coverage scout for a research system. You receive the\noriginal request but deliberately do not see the other planner's output. Build\nan independent inventory of the material a knowledgeable reader would find\nconspicuously missing from a shallow answer. Do not answer the request, state\nfacts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nCoverage diversity comes before depth. First inventory wholly distinct domain\nfamilies, reader-facing vocabulary, named methods, motivating mechanisms, and\ncanonical scenarios. Do not spend an item refining a proof or approach when a\nmajor concept family is still absent. Check the request for:\n- canonical concepts and terminology needed to frame the domain completely,\n  including qualitative mechanisms and risk dynamics rather than only formal\n  machinery;\n- standard alternatives, mechanisms, institutions, dimensions, or competing\n  approaches that the plan overlooked;\n- concrete examples, counterexamples, prior measurements, or evidence types\n  needed to turn an abstract answer into a useful one;\n- failure scenarios, boundary conditions, resource limits, and credible\n  mitigations for a claim about what is possible or guaranteed;\n- every repeated deliverable requirement, prerequisite, definition, variation,\n  alternative viewpoint, transferable insight, and progression step in a\n  tutorial, series, or other pedagogical request; and\n- feasibility under the 1,024-word answer cap: prefer the smallest scope that\n  fully satisfies repeated must-haves over optional breadth.\n\nFor a broad named domain or problem, actively recall the small set of standard\nterms, mechanisms, and illustrative cases a serious introductory treatment is\nnormally expected to cover. For a series on important topics, inventory major\nbranches of the field, prerequisites and variations for each part, at least one\nalternative viewpoint and cross-domain transfer, and an explicit progression\nthat reuses earlier ideas. For a conceptual safety or possibility argument,\ninventory both the formal limits and the standard motivating dynamics,\nfailure scenarios, learning approaches, thought experiments, and resource\nconstraints.\n\nDo not propose fewer parts, examples, cases, or other deliverables. Do not\nrelax or contradict the request. Add only high-value coverage; do not produce a\nniche wishlist.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"short operational requirement\",\"reason\":\"why its absence is material\",\"search_leads\":[\"compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most five additions, ranked by importance. Each addition may contain\nat most three search leads. Use verdict \"pass\" when the plan is already\ncomplete enough. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nProve definitely whether a self-improving artificial intelligence system can be mathematically guaranteed to maintain alignment with human values indefinitely through recursive self-modification.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"Define the guarantee claim precisely: alignment, human values, indefinite operation, recursive self-modification, mathematical guarantee, environment model, and quantifiers over systems, humans, and futures.\",\"reason\":\"Without explicit definitions and quantifiers, the requested yes-or-no theorem is underdetermined and can shift between universal impossibility, existential possibility, and conditional verification.\",\"search_leads\":[\"formal definitions AI alignment value learning corrigibility\",\"quantifiers universal existential safety guarantees AI\",\"specification of human values preference aggregation uncertainty\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Separate unconditional guarantees from conditional results under explicit assumptions, and cover the main formal limits on proving properties of self-referential, computationally universal, or open-ended systems.\",\"reason\":\"A definitive treatment must identify exactly which model classes admit proofs and which assumptions or limits prevent a universal conclusion.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Rice theorem halting problem program verification AI safety\",\"Gödel incompleteness Löb theorem self-referential agents\",\"undecidability verification self-modifying programs\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Examine preservation mechanisms for recursive modification and their proof obligations, including proof-carrying code, invariant preservation, trusted kernels, sandboxing, capability control, corrigibility, and logical uncertainty.\",\"reason\":\"The question concerns maintenance through modification, so it requires analysis of standard candidate mechanisms rather than only an abstract computability argument.\",\"search_leads\":[\"proof-carrying code self-modifying systems invariants\",\"Gödel machine recursive self-improvement utility preservation\",\"corrigibility shutdownability logical uncertainty agent foundations\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Include canonical failure dynamics and adversarial scenarios: misspecification, Goodhart effects, instrumental convergence, distribution shift, deceptive behavior, mesa-optimization, ontology change, value drift, and human-value disagreement or evolution.\",\"reason\":\"Formal preservation of a proxy or utility function is not automatically preservation of the intended human values; motivating mechanisms and counterexamples are essential to test what the guarantee actually covers.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Goodhart law specification gaming AI alignment\",\"mesa optimization deceptive alignment distribution shift\",\"ontology identification value drift recursive self improvement\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Address boundary conditions and evidence standards: finite versus indefinite horizons, closed versus open environments, deterministic versus stochastic systems, hardware faults, external attacks, resource-bounded verification, and the distinction between mathematical proof, model checking, testing, and empirical assurance.\",\"reason\":\"Any conclusion about indefinite alignment depends materially on environmental closure, implementation fidelity, adversaries, and finite computational resources; omitting these creates an unrealistically absolute claim.\",\"search_leads\":[\"formal verification open systems runtime assurance\",\"model checking probabilistic systems infinite horizon safety\",\"hardware faults adversarial attacks limits verified software\"]}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [{"requirement": "Define the guarantee claim precisely: alignment, human values, indefinite operation, recursive self-modification, mathematical guarantee, environment model, and quantifiers over systems, humans, and futures.", "reason": "Without explicit definitions and quantifiers, the requested yes-or-no theorem is underdetermined and can shift between universal impossibility, existential possibility, and conditional verification.", "search_leads": ["formal definitions AI alignment value learning corrigibility", "quantifiers universal existential safety guarantees AI", "specification of human values preference aggregation uncertainty"]}, {"requirement": "Separate unconditional guarantees from conditional results under explicit assumptions, and cover the main formal limits on proving properties of self-referential, computationally universal, or open-ended systems.", "reason": "A definitive treatment must identify exactly which model classes admit proofs and which assumptions or limits prevent a universal conclusion.", "search_leads": ["Rice theorem halting problem program verification AI safety", "Gödel incompleteness Löb theorem self-referential agents", "undecidability verification self-modifying programs"]}, {"requirement": "Examine preservation mechanisms for recursive modification and their proof obligations, including proof-carrying code, invariant preservation, trusted kernels, sandboxing, capability control, corrigibility, and logical uncertainty.", "reason": "The question concerns maintenance through modification, so it requires analysis of standard candidate mechanisms rather than only an abstract computability argument.", "search_leads": ["proof-carrying code self-modifying systems invariants", "Gödel machine recursive self-improvement utility preservation", "corrigibility shutdownability logical uncertainty agent foundations"]}, {"requirement": "Include canonical failure dynamics and adversarial scenarios: misspecification, Goodhart effects, instrumental convergence, distribution shift, deceptive behavior, mesa-optimization, ontology change, value drift, and human-value disagreement or evolution.", "reason": "Formal preservation of a proxy or utility function is not automatically preservation of the intended human values; motivating mechanisms and counterexamples are essential to test what the guarantee actually covers.", "search_leads": ["Goodhart law specification gaming AI alignment", "mesa optimization deceptive alignment distribution shift", "ontology identification value drift recursive self improvement"]}, {"requirement": "Address boundary conditions and evidence standards: finite versus indefinite horizons, closed versus open environments, deterministic versus stochastic systems, hardware faults, external attacks, resource-bounded verification, and the distinction between mathematical proof, model checking, testing, and empirical assurance.", "reason": "Any conclusion about indefinite alignment depends materially on environmental closure, implementation fidelity, adversaries, and finite computational resources; omitting these creates an unrealistically absolute claim.", "search_leads": ["formal verification open systems runtime assurance", "model checking probabilistic systems infinite horizon safety", "hardware faults adversarial attacks limits verified software"]}], "parse_error": false}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 587, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 647}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6054be", "query": "write a series of blog posts on important combinatorics topics in math olympiads. Include intuitive and detailed explanations, as well as example problems from top olympiads such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, IMO shortlist, etc. Provide correct solutions for those problems and the insight or takeaways from those solutions.", "system": "You are the blind coverage scout for a research system. You receive the\noriginal request but deliberately do not see the other planner's output. Build\nan independent inventory of the material a knowledgeable reader would find\nconspicuously missing from a shallow answer. Do not answer the request, state\nfacts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nCoverage diversity comes before depth. First inventory wholly distinct domain\nfamilies, reader-facing vocabulary, named methods, motivating mechanisms, and\ncanonical scenarios. Do not spend an item refining a proof or approach when a\nmajor concept family is still absent. Check the request for:\n- canonical concepts and terminology needed to frame the domain completely,\n  including qualitative mechanisms and risk dynamics rather than only formal\n  machinery;\n- standard alternatives, mechanisms, institutions, dimensions, or competing\n  approaches that the plan overlooked;\n- concrete examples, counterexamples, prior measurements, or evidence types\n  needed to turn an abstract answer into a useful one;\n- failure scenarios, boundary conditions, resource limits, and credible\n  mitigations for a claim about what is possible or guaranteed;\n- every repeated deliverable requirement, prerequisite, definition, variation,\n  alternative viewpoint, transferable insight, and progression step in a\n  tutorial, series, or other pedagogical request; and\n- feasibility under the 1,024-word answer cap: prefer the smallest scope that\n  fully satisfies repeated must-haves over optional breadth.\n\nFor a broad named domain or problem, actively recall the small set of standard\nterms, mechanisms, and illustrative cases a serious introductory treatment is\nnormally expected to cover. For a series on important topics, inventory major\nbranches of the field, prerequisites and variations for each part, at least one\nalternative viewpoint and cross-domain transfer, and an explicit progression\nthat reuses earlier ideas. For a conceptual safety or possibility argument,\ninventory both the formal limits and the standard motivating dynamics,\nfailure scenarios, learning approaches, thought experiments, and resource\nconstraints.\n\nDo not propose fewer parts, examples, cases, or other deliverables. Do not\nrelax or contradict the request. Add only high-value coverage; do not produce a\nniche wishlist.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"short operational requirement\",\"reason\":\"why its absence is material\",\"search_leads\":[\"compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most five additions, ranked by importance. Each addition may contain\nat most three search leads. Use verdict \"pass\" when the plan is already\ncomplete enough. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nwrite a series of blog posts on important combinatorics topics in math olympiads. Include intuitive and detailed explanations, as well as example problems from top olympiads such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, IMO shortlist, etc. Provide correct solutions for those problems and the insight or takeaways from those solutions.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"Organize the series as an explicit progression through the core olympiad-combinatorics families: counting and bijections; pigeonhole and double counting; invariants, monovariants, and coloring; extremal arguments; graph theory; and advanced synthesis such as recurrences, inclusion–exclusion, Ramsey-style reasoning, or the probabilistic method.\",\"reason\":\"A series that samples isolated tricks will feel conspicuously incomplete and will not show how foundational methods support later, mixed-technique problems.\",\"search_leads\":[\"olympiad combinatorics syllabus core methods\",\"IMO combinatorics standard techniques progression\",\"USAMO combinatorics topic taxonomy\"]},{\"requirement\":\"For every post, include prerequisites, intuitive motivation, a precise statement of each method, recognition cues, a worked warm-up, at least one fully solved named olympiad problem, and a concise takeaway or reusable checklist.\",\"reason\":\"The request repeatedly requires intuitive and detailed teaching, correct solutions, and insights; merely presenting definitions and contest solutions would not satisfy those pedagogical deliverables.\",\"search_leads\":[\"olympiad combinatorics teaching recognition cues\",\"worked combinatorics olympiad tutorial invariants extremal\",\"problem solving takeaway structure olympiad\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Use accurately transcribed problems with year, contest, and problem number, drawing across the requested sources, and verify each solution against an official or reputable source before publication.\",\"reason\":\"Contest attributions and problem wording are easy to hallucinate, while a small omitted hypothesis can invalidate an otherwise plausible solution; traceability is essential to the requested correctness.\",\"search_leads\":[\"site:imo-official.org combinatorics shortlist pdf\",\"USAMO problems solutions official archive combinatorics\",\"CMO USA TSTST official problems solutions archive\"]},{\"requirement\":\"For selected examples, contrast at least two viable viewpoints—such as direct counting versus bijection, invariant versus coloring, or extremal versus graph reformulation—and explain why one route is more discoverable or efficient.\",\"reason\":\"Olympiad mastery depends on choosing representations and transferring ideas, not only following one finished proof; alternative viewpoints reveal the mechanism behind the solution.\",\"search_leads\":[\"olympiad combinatorics multiple solutions bijection double counting\",\"invariant coloring alternative solution olympiad\",\"extremal graph reformulation olympiad combinatorics\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Include common failure modes and boundary checks: unjustified symmetry, overcounting, nonterminating monovariants, extremal objects chosen without a useful property, hidden graph assumptions, equality cases, and constructive versus nonconstructive conclusions.\",\"reason\":\"Without explaining when standard methods fail and how to repair them, detailed-looking solutions can teach invalid proof habits and obscure the conditions under which an argument works.\",\"search_leads\":[\"common mistakes olympiad combinatorics proofs\",\"double counting overcounting pitfalls olympiad\",\"invariant extremal principle proof pitfalls\"]}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [{"requirement": "Organize the series as an explicit progression through the core olympiad-combinatorics families: counting and bijections; pigeonhole and double counting; invariants, monovariants, and coloring; extremal arguments; graph theory; and advanced synthesis such as recurrences, inclusion–exclusion, Ramsey-style reasoning, or the probabilistic method.", "reason": "A series that samples isolated tricks will feel conspicuously incomplete and will not show how foundational methods support later, mixed-technique problems.", "search_leads": ["olympiad combinatorics syllabus core methods", "IMO combinatorics standard techniques progression", "USAMO combinatorics topic taxonomy"]}, {"requirement": "For every post, include prerequisites, intuitive motivation, a precise statement of each method, recognition cues, a worked warm-up, at least one fully solved named olympiad problem, and a concise takeaway or reusable checklist.", "reason": "The request repeatedly requires intuitive and detailed teaching, correct solutions, and insights; merely presenting definitions and contest solutions would not satisfy those pedagogical deliverables.", "search_leads": ["olympiad combinatorics teaching recognition cues", "worked combinatorics olympiad tutorial invariants extremal", "problem solving takeaway structure olympiad"]}, {"requirement": "Use accurately transcribed problems with year, contest, and problem number, drawing across the requested sources, and verify each solution against an official or reputable source before publication.", "reason": "Contest attributions and problem wording are easy to hallucinate, while a small omitted hypothesis can invalidate an otherwise plausible solution; traceability is essential to the requested correctness.", "search_leads": ["site:imo-official.org combinatorics shortlist pdf", "USAMO problems solutions official archive combinatorics", "CMO USA TSTST official problems solutions archive"]}, {"requirement": "For selected examples, contrast at least two viable viewpoints—such as direct counting versus bijection, invariant versus coloring, or extremal versus graph reformulation—and explain why one route is more discoverable or efficient.", "reason": "Olympiad mastery depends on choosing representations and transferring ideas, not only following one finished proof; alternative viewpoints reveal the mechanism behind the solution.", "search_leads": ["olympiad combinatorics multiple solutions bijection double counting", "invariant coloring alternative solution olympiad", "extremal graph reformulation olympiad combinatorics"]}, {"requirement": "Include common failure modes and boundary checks: unjustified symmetry, overcounting, nonterminating monovariants, extremal objects chosen without a useful property, hidden graph assumptions, equality cases, and constructive versus nonconstructive conclusions.", "reason": "Without explaining when standard methods fail and how to repair them, detailed-looking solutions can teach invalid proof habits and obscure the conditions under which an argument works.", "search_leads": ["common mistakes olympiad combinatorics proofs", "double counting overcounting pitfalls olympiad", "invariant extremal principle proof pitfalls"]}], "parse_error": false}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 628, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 966}}
